Given an array of elements of length n, ranging from 0 to n - 1. All elements may not be present in the array. If the element is not present then there will be -1 present in the array. Rearrange the array such that arr[i] = i and if i is not present, store -1 at that place.Examples:Input: arr[] = [-1, -1, 6, 1, 9, 3, 2, -1, 4, -1]Output: [-1, 1, 2, 3, 4, -1, 6, -1, -1, 9]Explanation: In range 0 to 9, all except 0, 5, 7 and 8 are present. Hence, we print -1 instead of them.Input: arr[] = [0, 1, 2, 3, 4, 5] Output: [0, 1, 2, 3, 4, 5]Explanation: In range 0 to 5, all number are present.Try It YourselfTable of Content[Naive Approach] Rearranging Array Elements – O(n²) Time and O(1) Space[Better Approach] Using Auxiliary Array - O(n) Time and O(n) Space[Expected Approach] In-Place Rearrangement by Swapping – O(n) Time and O(1) Space[Naive Approach] Rearranging Array Elements – O(n²) Time and O(1) SpaceThe Idea is to place each element i at index i if it exists in the array. For every index i, linearly search the entire array to check whether the value i is present. If found, move i to index i by swapping. If i is not present in the array, assign -1 to index i. Repeat this process for all indices to obtain the final rearranged array.
            C++

    #include <iostream>
#include <vector>
using namespace std;

void modifyArray(vector<int>& arr) {
    int n = arr.size();

    // Move i to arr[i] if present
    for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
        for (int j = 0; j < n; j++) {
            if (arr[j] == i) {
                swap(arr[i], arr[j]);
                break;
            }
        }
    }

    // Put -1 for places where i is
    // not present
    for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
        if (arr[i] != i) {
            arr[i] = -1;
        }
    }
}


int main() {
    vector<int> arr = { -1, -1, 6, 1, 9, 3, 2, -1, 4, -1 };
    modifyArray(arr);
    for (int i = 0; i < arr.size(); i++) {
        cout << arr[i] << " ";
    }
    return 0;
}


